WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.738
A deployment command finishing does not
always mean the application is healthy.

2
00:00:07.818 --> 00:00:13.459
A rollout may stall, pods may crash,
readiness probes may fail,

3
00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:20.351
traffic may not reach the new version, or
only part of the change may succeed.

4
00:00:20.431 --> 00:00:27.562
A script that reports success too early
can create false confidence and trigger

5
00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:32.751
later automation before the workload is
actually ready.

6
00:00:32.831 --> 00:00:40.601
Secure deployment scripts should wait for
expected rollout and readiness conditions

7
00:00:40.681 --> 00:00:42.703
that fit the workload.

8
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:48.743
They should use timeouts, detect partial
or stalled deployments,

9
00:00:48.823 --> 00:00:51.484
and report failures clearly.

10
00:00:51.564 --> 00:00:58.163
Health checks should be appropriate to the
application and environment.

11
00:00:58.243 --> 00:01:05.374
For some workloads, Kubernetes readiness
is enough for the deployment script.

12
00:01:05.454 --> 00:01:13.011
For others, additional smoke checks or
monitoring signals may be part of the release

13
00:01:13.091 --> 00:01:13.943
process.

14
00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:18.706
Failure handling should avoid misleading
success.

15
00:01:18.786 --> 00:01:21.021
If a rollout check fails,

16
00:01:21.101 --> 00:01:27.274
the script should not continue with later
promotion, notification,

17
00:01:27.354 --> 00:01:30.654
or cleanup steps that imply success.

18
00:01:30.734 --> 00:01:38.610
Operators should receive a clear status,
target, version, and follow-up expectation.

19
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:45.502
The script should make it easy to see
whether a failure was during rendering,

20
00:01:45.582 --> 00:01:51.755
apply, rollout, health verification, or a
later operational check.

21
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:56.731
Rollback planning matters before
deployment begins.

22
00:01:56.811 --> 00:02:01.282
Teams should know what rollback can and
cannot do,

23
00:02:01.362 --> 00:02:08.386
what stateful resources require extra
care, and who owns recovery decisions.

24
00:02:08.466 --> 00:02:14.001
A stateless workload may be easier to
revert than a migration,

25
00:02:14.081 --> 00:02:19.403
persistent volume change, or external
dependency change.

26
00:02:19.483 --> 00:02:26.508
Secure deployment automation treats
rollback as part of the deployment plan,

27
00:02:26.588 --> 00:02:30.100
not a wish made after something breaks.
